#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d462c4fb-c784-5dc3-adc1-1cd562c6a55e" :question/math-academy-id "e-84" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x+4)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "ace1e5ac-fecc-559d-923d-9fec38f410ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-85" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "43990279-e10b-53dc-8f18-533f3962a935" :question/math-academy-id "e-86" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2x+2} & =\\frac{x(x+1)}{2(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+1)}}{2\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/eda5457edafe14fb51fcc75ca75677b199bfc3e0aef8940c66f8aa78cf8cd5c3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3b1c0748-807c-5a77-b9e8-62ce5eb03c78" :question/math-academy-id "e-87" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{(x+1)}{x(x+1)}+\\frac{x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1+x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8f2183-c2ce-5e5c-ba85-e1142b6e8cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280135} {:answer/id #uuid "f4617fa1-f49e-5ee3-9701-3c921ef021c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280136} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1927f8-eba4-5e53-8942-a620442e7454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280137} {:answer/id #uuid "32d0bb0c-dd8a-56a1-8e0e-294257c95301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280138} {:answer/id #uuid "266e6b07-02d9-5aab-98cc-886a115be5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8f2183-c2ce-5e5c-ba85-e1142b6e8cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280135} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a3b7d8e-efdc-5f74-9437-1074f22c782b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93131c03-4409-5728-ae70-037a4d62a977" :question/math-academy-id "q-107032" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12} & =\\frac{3x(x-3)}{4(x-3)}=\\frac{3x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/d1f0db6bba6e5bbd0079ae06a9f5199eaf8f24c4dfe0890ee691e2a5afcafc30.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b957a2dd-d89d-54fd-9fd9-8d48982962a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280142} {:answer/id #uuid "454847af-3c11-5d46-95e0-39ff849fe44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280143} {:answer/id #uuid "dc146a87-85e8-5792-a760-ffc79dad269d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280144} {:answer/id #uuid "17bc5a6b-2555-5470-8d6c-b10ea6a7a4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280145} {:answer/id #uuid "ae870a6e-3fa5-5a61-9bb8-42d9d952ccbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b957a2dd-d89d-54fd-9fd9-8d48982962a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280142} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c71ed43-ea17-58b5-900a-ee95d6304c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6e307a2-79a3-5f77-9f0e-1598db1ed452" :question/math-academy-id "q-107068" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x} & =\\frac{x(x+4)({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{x(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x(x+4)}({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{\\cancel{x(x+4)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/a10cef51ad5955f6065fbf4deaccbab1cda1f576db96310236ea976bb8d8eb8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ed4443-3771-50f6-b284-f540a89ebaa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280149} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6214c7-c047-5571-918f-806e66367cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280150} {:answer/id #uuid "1b180fba-b286-597c-848a-ecd7a6c8bb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280151} {:answer/id #uuid "5585a7ca-2dc5-5f58-87ba-bfd458b1c17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280152} {:answer/id #uuid "557ee11b-1196-5a8d-a454-92f0b8a444d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ed4443-3771-50f6-b284-f540a89ebaa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280149} :answer-field/id #uuid "91349b59-7134-5667-a560-f319c0e88525" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "248881bd-12e3-5bbe-9742-1e786c69f660" :question/math-academy-id "q-107074" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{(2{x}^{2}+1)-({x}^{2}+3)}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation has no real solutions, therefore $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe466a23-c6fe-5259-be76-7706806a4b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280156} {:answer/id #uuid "de50e952-8725-53a0-8b0d-f0bc581fe47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de50e952-8725-53a0-8b0d-f0bc581fe47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280157} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3851eaa-4b73-5340-8c90-12d044fed340" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ba1c34-45da-5976-b370-8f8ed7d7f89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339757" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3} & =\\frac{7}{4(x+3)}+\\frac{5}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+5(4)}{4(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69b8c7d-78cb-50c6-a3f0-3c7b16369b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c69b8c7d-78cb-50c6-a3f0-3c7b16369b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280160} :answer-field/id #uuid "c062f8f2-f8e5-54ab-8ca9-047e3f359d3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2085814-78d7-55fd-bc67-38e63d4c9599" :question/math-academy-id "q-339763" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6} & =\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(2)-3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2(4x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n2(4x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cade2cb1-3a31-5320-a2d0-529fb4a4001c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cade2cb1-3a31-5320-a2d0-529fb4a4001c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280163} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc87fae7-f9f2-599a-af68-66769c52ea50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64095e03-4823-54bd-846a-918e3528bd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64095e03-4823-54bd-846a-918e3528bd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280165} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbf238b-7e20-5377-9a4e-a28bf608f9ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280164} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff913e87-db88-52f6-a3e6-4787bcef2876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff913e87-db88-52f6-a3e6-4787bcef2876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280167} :answer-field/id #uuid "38415c84-a7e3-5f5b-9db2-87c7b7fe9325" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fedc34c5-d7a5-5801-9c83-0ce6f45aaef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339779" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,$\n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x(x+5)-({x}^{2}-9)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-5$,  $x=-3,$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ad7813-4270-59b7-9380-442a2c52b5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ad7813-4270-59b7-9380-442a2c52b5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280170} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a398d7-d681-5cb3-9d82-8663743fac65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4c20995-f4be-5507-9906-2fc4d1f236c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4c20995-f4be-5507-9906-2fc4d1f236c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280172} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be7f74c-4752-510d-8220-6e02cb9ba388" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abc06c45-2df3-5a27-9dad-f25ba334ab5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339947" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(3-x)(3+x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(3-x)(3+x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,3\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/ead2334ba9281edbe8545c4d18b8e64bb72e28915d98674fe68364dfdc4e0a4e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5db644-b43e-5270-a3a7-bea4f3d93efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} {:answer/id #uuid "9778a7b0-25bf-57c3-8a51-1f1186c366c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5db644-b43e-5270-a3a7-bea4f3d93efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be7f9bc-b435-5197-9995-11c6c7f803ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1ccfc4-87aa-5658-a4e9-e4fd91312f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1ccfc4-87aa-5658-a4e9-e4fd91312f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b0f90bd-f8aa-574a-a366-b1e339f3485a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d94d162d-c46c-566a-8df0-7c2509808895" :question/math-academy-id "q-339951" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{(x-2)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+7)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-7,\\,x=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/069b968e405a1302f67a085da7aff92979c95d4fca423aad3885ca6267d8cc34.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c44301-edd4-5f24-91a6-6af2cc80d3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44c44301-edd4-5f24-91a6-6af2cc80d3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280181} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f129fe-fa2b-5846-b334-35af1aebc21d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b8a916-a195-5e48-b50c-510ef6cc92a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39b8a916-a195-5e48-b50c-510ef6cc92a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280183} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdadc70f-39b7-5af5-842d-f7c154d28196" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa3567c-9137-58ac-b2ca-e555c0991f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339953" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x-5}{(x-7)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,x=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=7,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/56b3d93afca64c37cd48013702f2317f41935e85d5b795c34f31a76927852d44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5660f26-e5fd-5046-a763-c39d7e6a2f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5660f26-e5fd-5046-a763-c39d7e6a2f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280186} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c6390b-6c28-57b3-a1b9-b88e20a4f1aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "399f0d67-1eb9-57db-a89e-719174076197" :question/math-academy-id "q-339960" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12} & =\\frac{4(x-2)}{2(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{(x-2)}}{2(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d3780f-cd43-5899-9334-a4e849304d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280189} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d29871-5f53-514f-86eb-12c448d859ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d3780f-cd43-5899-9334-a4e849304d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280189} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe1a369-1e4d-591d-9265-7d6eb297aa60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "469b9897-9d0c-516a-93de-61de2ed705a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339971" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}(x+4)}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+4)}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9643f39a-af86-5403-bd4b-40bed5569f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280193} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7869e3-8f98-5186-962f-b4b9d4d83767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9643f39a-af86-5403-bd4b-40bed5569f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280193} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7136970-f63a-5d20-948b-d530596f642f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff284a84-3518-5c5c-aee6-4a46c4366526" :question/math-academy-id "q-339976" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10} & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-4)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{2}^{2})}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)(x+2)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x+5)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1740fd6-4d2e-51ff-a05d-1dcc4db7de94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118492} {:answer/id #uuid "072e1440-4e2e-5f6e-b5ca-cea45d917f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118493} {:answer/id #uuid "17268b30-9e44-5741-a68f-8287126fd086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118494} {:answer/id #uuid "490bc6ae-61af-5c5b-a85d-f3ea93884dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118495} {:answer/id #uuid "789c1988-1b4e-5ca9-8a05-2e6e71e41d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e1440-4e2e-5f6e-b5ca-cea45d917f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118493} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98d390d-cf46-5645-9202-e2d155bbb1c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "626def15-9d74-5450-b279-13015c52f2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37066" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12} & =\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-4x-3x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{x(x-4)-3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{(x-4)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-3)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44faa13f-ac1d-5d1b-9aa6-fc59240d2e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280197} {:answer/id #uuid "649bcf2b-3996-5f0e-8b86-ac96581c94c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280198} {:answer/id #uuid "5357c30f-ca28-5534-9542-a5f0c2885800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280199} {:answer/id #uuid "49a04468-d369-5d0f-9938-37d171867b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280200} {:answer/id #uuid "50d19829-9542-5ff1-8ca3-3e2e1414532a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5357c30f-ca28-5534-9542-a5f0c2885800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280199} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec21cfe9-7452-5681-9fd2-68af08dcd5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c3564f2-172b-5ce6-973c-55bb62901f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37069" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{12x}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f5495f-cd93-5445-8029-45e0927eb692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118906} {:answer/id #uuid "04a6e221-63ee-5a6a-8b5e-cc6b6ee6ad0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118907} {:answer/id #uuid "07a7cfea-910a-5517-969e-7a8988a6f7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118908} {:answer/id #uuid "282aa386-b32b-588a-8326-a11c4354e219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118909} {:answer/id #uuid "2891c8ed-6db5-548d-a4ee-c025fd2f07bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a7cfea-910a-5517-969e-7a8988a6f7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118908} :answer-field/id #uuid "9670d1f0-9da2-5e3e-a3fe-875058b24fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be65cb0b-274b-58ab-a00c-2f7da80461c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-4631" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/c76cce145059193c5c5348f2de347ac470f35e8b5392e196ac8b9766382bf2b8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b5a260-ac60-52ea-a55c-34f8f616a856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280204} {:answer/id #uuid "155cbef5-d4bf-5d19-9119-2b6c4f607dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280205} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc32ec7-8a46-508f-87ef-c55ea86bd785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280206} {:answer/id #uuid "370f7a98-302d-5a74-8f4d-c2d5a1d408df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280207} {:answer/id #uuid "92fffbe3-63fa-51f0-954e-96df25de2048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "155cbef5-d4bf-5d19-9119-2b6c4f607dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280205} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28c8f37-1cb0-51be-b67f-af017d0511c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53858ba9-755e-54c6-83a7-797ed7120dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47853" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-6)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-6)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=6$ and $x=-1$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/b3d4116e1157d2f9b5414a015965b371982663518d04631810f19d8be4a7de93.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b3dec0-1f05-5c68-9512-bdebc2a567fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280211} {:answer/id #uuid "98948942-15fd-517d-810b-d0c9bd4b0be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280212} {:answer/id #uuid "de41d696-daab-5b8e-b788-fc554e5810f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280213} {:answer/id #uuid "b655f1ec-64ce-56c1-83c3-6c678e031c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280214} {:answer/id #uuid "d50dd59c-e9e5-56f4-a0fb-4e4cfb60de89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b3dec0-1f05-5c68-9512-bdebc2a567fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280211} :answer-field/id #uuid "4018887a-b7dc-5e26-88f3-652e88837dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2c4ac97-8cea-5552-8956-b9b9e4733346" :question/math-academy-id "q-47854" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1674388-12a5-5e6a-a202-8505f299e592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280218} {:answer/id #uuid "e3580144-80cd-57e6-8cab-a52ad9fcb289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280219} {:answer/id #uuid "4d037fec-3ae7-5727-b1c3-1e4cb1ec0f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280220} {:answer/id #uuid "4c60c103-0f6a-5911-956f-920df2b3fdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280221} {:answer/id #uuid "79d15cd6-67a4-54e6-b880-1e7db7a582d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1674388-12a5-5e6a-a202-8505f299e592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280218} :answer-field/id #uuid "a526f5cc-91f2-57ef-928c-83ed219915f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a320ace-3bfe-55b8-b3d5-e7e54548d7c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-68151" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+1)}{3(2x+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/7ca0a626369d11d7d3cd8e2d99cbfb79eb37c534b86a1df3f43aaeff5fb932e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ff823d-86fd-5c48-99dd-36815fa559aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280225} {:answer/id #uuid "5156ad7b-3dab-55ee-8611-a179825c8720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280226} {:answer/id #uuid "79dee673-ac97-5b5f-b503-49ac24984313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280227} {:answer/id #uuid "a3de4496-1426-5738-931a-d133a42e61d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280228} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7ce91b-3f31-59b9-8bbc-63367e2a0c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ff823d-86fd-5c48-99dd-36815fa559aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280225} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89f241d-771d-5848-b5f8-7a5814b68cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ad4d892-a9fd-5ddc-8924-336d5a9c9793" :question/math-academy-id "q-68167" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2{x}^{2}}{18-12x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-2{x}^{2}}{18-12x} & =\\frac{x(3-2x)}{6(3-2x)}=\\frac{x}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/04c9f0c0e0f8ee16e9eee949d23a816b9ad877ab205d3231bb183a486da97544.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8543c43-5dba-5168-86bd-b44902bb247d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280232} {:answer/id #uuid "280c662c-e7a0-5783-a106-4cb469cacfeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=7$ and $x=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280233} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a11b4b-21ea-5e95-856c-e75c56b18d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280234} {:answer/id #uuid "792fb9ce-b927-53a3-9466-2593121ec093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280235} {:answer/id #uuid "ead825aa-02ba-5e59-94e1-b75331cf84e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "280c662c-e7a0-5783-a106-4cb469cacfeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=7$ and $x=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280233} :answer-field/id #uuid "5483eb3c-d445-54c8-83c3-9f3530808bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0738580-739a-541e-96ef-d435fb22b7bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-68177" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x+5+x-7}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2x-2}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2(x-1)}{(x-7)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=7\\,,\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=7$ and $x=-5.$"}]}
